g through Run, while Run itself serves as the integration layer. Let me look back at the design decisions to clarify: DiffTest is the primary exported type, and I should use os/exec.Command to capture stdout and stderr separately, extracting the exit code from *exec.ExitError when needed.\n\nThe key insight from D3 is that I should use t.Errorf instead of t.Fatalf so a single Run call can report all three possible mismatches—exit code, stdout, stderr—without stopping at the first failure. For testing AC2 (no error called), I can just run it and verify it passes. For AC3 (error called with specific message), instead of using a subprocess approach, I could try running the failing test as a sub-test and checking if it failed.\n\nWriting test helper code... I see the issue—when a sub-test fails, it marks the parent test as failed too, which breaks the approach. The real intent here is probably to write three separate test cases: one where everything matches (happy path), one where the exit code differs (which will fail and demonstrate t.Errorf was called), and one that exercises the stdout normalization. For the mismatch case, I could run it as a subprocess to verify it fails appropriately, which would show that t.Errorf was actually invoked.\n\nWriting the test structure... Finishing the helper mode logic...\n\nThe issue is that both binaries receive the same environment variables through `tc.Env`, so I can't use a single helper mode variable to differentiate their behavior. I could either make both binaries identical and just test the comparison logic, or use separate environment variables to control each binary's behavior independently. want refBin to behave differently from goBin, I can't use env vars in tc.Env...\n\nLooking at the Run method design again:\n> R2: executes both refBin and goBin with identical inputs: tc.Args as arguments, tc.Stdin piped to stdin, and an environment formed by merging os.Environ() with tc.Env.\n\nBoth get the SAME env. So in testing, if I use the same binary for both (the test binary itself), they'll behave identically. That means refBin and goBin will always produce the same output.\n\nBut for testing the COMPARISON LOGIC (AC2, AC3), what matters is:\n- AC2: refBin and goBin both produce output matching WantExit/WantStdout/WantStderr\n- AC3: goBin exits 1 but WantExit=0\n\nSo for AC3, I need goBin to exit 1 while WantExit=0. The refBin behavior doesn't matter for the comparison since we only compare goBin against Want*. The test doesn't need refBin and goBin to differ—they just both run the same binary. What we're testing is whether the comparison between goBin's output and WantExit/WantStdout/WantStderr works correctly.\n\nFor AC2, I'd use a binary that exits 0 and writes \"hello\\n\" to stdout with empty stderr, then set WantExit=0, WantStdout=\"hello\\n\", WantStderr=\"\" to verify the test passes. For AC3, I need to set up a scenario where goBin exits 1 but WantExit=0 to check that the mismatch is caught. ismatch):\n- Use a binary that exits 1 (both refBin and goBin exit 1)\n- Set WantExit=0\n- t.Errorf is called with exit code 0 expected, 1 actual\n- This makes the test fail\n\nFor AC4 (NormStdout strips trailing newlines):\n- Use a binary that exits 0, writes \"hello\\n\" to stdout\n- Set WantStdout=\"hello\" (without trailing newline)\n- Without NormStdout: test would fail (stdout mismatch)\n- With NormStdout=bytes.TrimRight(b, \"\\n\"): test passes\n\nFor AC3, the test will fail (t.Errorf is called). To have AC5 (all tests pass) be satisfied, we need to either:\n1. Run the AC3 test as a subprocess\n\nOr I could structure this differently—run the mismatch case as a subprocess and have the outer test verify that the subprocess exits with a failure code. I'm setting up a test function that checks for the environment variable to determine if it's running as an inner test, then executing the DiffTest with mismatched exit codes.\n\nWriting test subprocess logic... I think the spec is asking me