orrectly identify itself as a helper and exit with code 1 as expected. HELPER` not set, so it continues to `m.Run()`. When the inner test runs with `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch`, it executes DiffTest with `WantExit=0` but spawns a subprocess with `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER=exit1`, which causes TestMain to exit with code 1. Back in DiffTest.Run, the mismatch between the expected exit code (0) and actual (1) triggers an error.\n\nThe inner test fails and the subprocess exits non-zero, which the outer test detects. The output contains both \"0\" and \"1\" as expected, so the outer test passes. I realize there's a subtle issue though—when running the test binary directly, I need to use `os.Args[0]` as the command rather than invoking `go test`.\n\nI'm also thinking through how TestMain works here. When DiffTest.Run executes the binary with just `tc.Args` (no `-test.run` flags), TestMain runs first and checks for the helper environment variable. If `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER` is set, it handles the exit code directly without calling `m.Run()`, so no actual tests execute—it just exits with the desired code.\n\nRefining the TestMain implementation...\n\nStill writing TestMain logic... run=^TestRun_ExitCodeMismatch$\", \"-test.v\")\n```\n\nThe subprocess runs TestRun_ExitCodeMismatch again with `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch`. Inside that test, DiffTest.Run executes the reference binary, but since refBin and goBin both point to os.Args[0] (the test binary itself), it ends up running the test binary twice—once as the reference and once as the actual binary. Both invocations get `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER=exit1` from tc.Env merged with the current environment.\n\nWhen DiffTest.Run invokes os.Args[0] as a subprocess, TestMain checks for `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER` first and exits early in helper mode without running the actual tests. The subprocess environment will contain both `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch` and `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER=exit1`, but since TestMain prioritizes the helper check,