nThe subprocess returns empty stdout and stderr with exit code 1. I should also verify that running `go test` with specific test filters won't introduce coverage instrumentation or other flags that could interfere with the behavior.\n\nFor the outer test, I'm setting `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch` in the subprocess environment, which makes the inner check pass and runs `TestRun_ExitCodeMismatch`.\n\nNow I'm realizing that `t.Parallel()` in the inner test is fine—it just marks the test as parallelizable, but since only this one test runs in the subprocess, it executes alone anyway. The design looks solid, so I'm ready to write the implementation.\n\nI need to be careful about how I handle `WantStdout` and `WantStderr` being `nil` versus `[]byte{}`. When the subprocess writes nothing to stdout, `bytes.Buffer.Bytes()` returns an empty non-nil slice, so I should initialize these fields as `[]byte{}` in the tests to match that behavior. Both nil and empty slices work fine with bytes.Equal since they compare equal, so there's no issue there. For stdin, passing nil to bytes.NewReader creates a reader that immediately returns EOF, which correctly represents no input per the spec. Now I'm ready to write the final implementation, though I need to check what packages testutils is allowed to import.\n\nThe implementation only uses standard library packages—bytes, fmt, os, os/exec, and testing—which all fall within the allowed imports. I should be careful with the nil comparison for WantStdout and handle it properly when writing the actual code. Writing the test helper code...\n\nActually, I realize `runHelper` always calls `os.Exit()` in every code path, so the function never returns normally. The compiler won't complain about the unreachable `os.Exit(m.Run())` that follows it in `TestMain`, even though that line will never execute—it's just dead code that's harmless.\n\nNow I need to verify that when the subprocess test runs with `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER` set, it doesn't accidentally trigger helper mode.  The subprocess inherits the parent's environment variables, so `GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch` gets passed down, but since `GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER` isn't set in the subprocess, TestMain correctly skips helper mode and runs the actual test, which then executes the inner case logic with the helper environment variable merged in for the next level.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a potential issue with `os.Args[0]` when the subprocess is spawned—the test binary path might differ between the parent and subprocess contexts, which could affect how the binary is invoked.\n\nActually, thinking through this more carefully: when I use `exec.Command(os.Args[0], ...)` in the outer test, the subprocess receives that same binary path as its `os.Args[0]`. Then when the inner subprocess runs and also uses `os.Args[0]`, it's pointing to the same test binary executable, so the chain of invocations should work correctly.\n\nI'm satisfied the design is sound—time to implement it, and I need to verify the test file location.\n\nThe test file should go in `package testutils` rather than `package testutils_test` since it needs to access unexported types and constants, which will make the implementation cleaner. Now I'm ready to write out the actual code structure: the main difftest.go file with the DiffTest struct, its Run method, and the supporting binaryResult struct.\n\nWriting difftest.go implementation... I'm deciding whether to extract the environment merging logic into a separate function or just inline it since it only appears once in the code. Inlining keeps things simpler, so I'll skip the helper function and write the final version of difftest.go.\n\nNow I'm writing out the package documentation and the DiffTest struct definition, which describes the differential testing harness for comparing Go utility implementations against reference GNU binaries. I'm defining normalization functions for stdout and stderr that can strip things like trailing newlines or timestamps before comparison, then implementing the Run method that executes both binaries with identical inputs and reports any divergence in exit codes or output through test errors. Writing test assertions...\n\nNow I'm defining a struct to hold the results from running a binary, then setting up a function that executes a binary with given arguments and environment variables, capturing its output and exit code. I'm second-guessing whether the `//nolint:gocritic` comment is even necessary here—gocritic might not actually flag the direct append of `os.Environ()` since it returns a fresh slice each time. Let me skip the comment for now and add it only if the linter complains during testing. The safer approach would be to explicitly allocate a new slice with enough capacity and copy the base environment before appending, but that feels overly verbose for what's essentially a simple operation.\n\nNow I'm checking the test setup—when `tc.Stdin` is nil, `bytes.NewReader(nil)` works fine since the reader just returns EOF immediately. I'm also verifying the structure of the test result being built in `runBinary`. I'm verifying the imports needed for the test file—`bytes`, `fmt`, `os`, `os/exec`, `strings`, and `testing` are all standard library packages and everything checks out.\n\nNow I'm writing out the final implementation, though I need to reconsider how to handle output in the test helper. Using `fmt.Fprint` with blank identifiers to ignore the error is cleaner than trying to use `WriteString` directly, so I'll go with that approach for writing to stdout.\n\nI should also be mindful of the project's linting setup—I noticed the existing code uses `//nolint:gochecknoglobals`, which means golangci-lint is configured with gochecknoglobals enabled, so I'll need to be careful about package-level variables in my implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":176,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20086,"cache_read_input_tok