{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DnWnaJezhdA6hnAYXtqDPj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to write the test file. The key challenge is:\n1. AC2: Test that completes Run without calling t.Errorf (test passes)\n2. AC3: Test that t.Errorf is called with expected/actual exit codes when there's a mismatch\n3. AC4: Test that NormStdout hook works\n4. AC5: All tests must pass (green)\n\nFor AC3, I need the exit code mismatch test to not fail the outer test. I'll use the subprocess pattern.\n\nFor TestRun_AllMatch:\n- Use test binary itself (os.Args[0]) as both binaries\n\n\n- Set GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER=hello via Env\n- WantExit=0, WantStdout=[]byte(\"hello\\n\"), WantStderr=[]byte{}\n\nFor TestRun_ExitCodeMismatch:\n- Outer test: runs subprocess that has GO_TESTUTILS_INNER=exit_mismatch\n- Inner test: runs DiffTest with GO_TESTUTILS_HELPER=exit1, WantExit=0\n- Outer test verifies subprocess exits non-zero and output contains right content\n\nFor TestRun_NormStdout, I'll set up a similar subprocess pattern with the NormStdout hook configured.\n\nNow I'm checking how bytes.Buffer behaves when empty—specifically whether Bytes() returns nil or an empty slice. Looking at the implementation, when nothing's been written, the buffer's underlying slice is nil and the offset is 0, so slicing from that position gives nil rather than panicking.\n\nThis means both `nil` and `[]byte{}` work equivalently in my test since bytes.Equal treats them the same way—both convert to empty strings when compared. I'll use nil for clarity when expecting empty output.\n\nNow I'm writing the test file, being careful about how the subprocess handles the `-test.run` flag. When the outer test spawns the binary with a specific test filter, only that inner test runs, which then calls DiffTest to verify the behavior. I'm about to write out the test file. One thing I should verify: will calling `t.Parallel()` in the inner test cause problems? Since we're only running that single test in the subprocess with `-test.run=^TestRun_ExitCodeMismatch$`, marking it as parallel should be fine—the test framework will just mark it safe for concurrency and run it immediately without waiting for other tests.\n\nBut I need to be careful about where I place the `t.Parallel()` call. If I call it before checking the environment variable for the inner test case, it might interfere with the inner test's execution. I should restructure so that `t.Parallel()` only gets called in the outer test path, keeping the inner test case clean and avoiding any potential issues with the test framework's pause-and-resume behavior.\n\nNow I'll write out the complete test implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":226,"cache_read_input_tokens":88413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":226},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbeab3b-928b-423d-93c9-30066bce8854","uuid":"5976346f-dff3-4299-a029-e1e72f5e2c58"}
